narrative:
## Interfaces Across Module Boundaries

An interface is a contract. That contract can be defined in one module and satisfied by types in ANOTHER module. The compiler checks satisfaction at the call site — it doesn't care which module the type is defined in.

### The Interface Module

```xiom
module logging {
  pub interface Logger {
    fn log(msg: Str);
  }

  pub fn process[T: Logger](logger: &T, data: Str) {
    logger.log("Processing: " + data);
  }
}
```

### Implementation Module

```xiom
module console_backend {
  pub type ConsoleLogger = {
    prefix: Str;
  }

  pub fn ConsoleLogger.log(self, msg: Str) {
    io.println(self.prefix + " " + msg);
  }
}
```

Wait — `log` is a private method by default in the module. But for the interface to work across modules, the method needs to be accessible. In XIOM's structural typing, if a type HAS the method (regardless of module), it satisfies the interface.

### Using Cross-Module Interfaces

```xiom
use logging.Logger;
use logging.process;
use console_backend.ConsoleLogger;

fn main() -> Int {
  let logger = ConsoleLogger{ prefix: "[APP]" };
  process(&logger, "Hello World");
  return 0;
}
```

### Another Implementation in a Separate Module

```xiom
module file_backend {
  pub type FileLogger = {
    stored: Vec[Str];
  }

  pub fn new_file_logger() -> FileLogger {
    return FileLogger{ stored: Vec[Str].new() };
  }

  pub fn FileLogger.log(self: &mut FileLogger, msg: Str) {
    self.stored.push(msg);
  }

  pub fn FileLogger.get_logs(self) -> Vec[Str] {
    return self.stored;
  }
}
```

### The Architecture

```
logging module (interface + generic function)
    |
    +-- console_backend module (implements Logger)
    |
    +-- file_backend module (implements Logger)
```

Neither backend module imports the other. They only know about the Logger interface. The `logging` module doesn't know about either backend — it only knows the interface. This is **dependency inversion**: high-level modules depend on abstractions (interfaces), not low-level details (concrete types).

